## Class product

The room builds one shared kind of slice: a small web page that records a single business request and shows a pass or fail against two acceptance checks. Each learner uses their own topic (a refund request, a campaign intake, a supplier exception) with the same page shape. The facilitator supplies framework.sdd.works Lite skills and rules. Learners run those skills. They do not author a new skill on this day.

## Module 1. What SDD is, and what sits beside it

- Time: 50 minutes
- Course objectives: LO1, LO2
- SDD means the spec is the source the agent and the person both use to judge the work.
- Agentic programming means an agent takes steps toward that spec, including edits and tool calls.
- Harness engineering means the skills, rules, checks, and approvals that keep those steps inside the spec.
- Learners write a definition of SDD, then mark one line in a sample spec as checkable or not checkable.
- They draw the three-part map on one page: spec, agent, harness.
- They label one failure they have seen: a missing spec, an unbounded agent, or a missing check.

## Module 2. The four cores

- Time: 40 minutes
- Course objective: LO3
- Skills are repeatable jobs the agent can run.
- Rules are constraints that stay on for every job.
- Knowledge is the project facts the agent must use and must not invent.
- Workflow is the order of jobs, including who approves a step.
- Learners fill a four-row card for the class product. Each row names one example they will touch later in the day.

## Module 3. Agent, MCP, RAG, and application

- Time: 45 minutes
- Course objective: LO4
- An agent decides and acts inside a job.
- An MCP server exposes tools and data to that agent through a standard connection.
- A RAG flow retrieves passages from a known set and answers from those passages.
- An application is the product a person uses. It may call an agent, an MCP server, or neither.
- The facilitator gives six short requests. Learners sort each request into one box and say why.
- The close is four definitions in the learner's words, tied to the class product: the app is the page, the agent runs the skills, MCP is named as the later way to attach a tool, and RAG is named as out of scope for this day.

## Module 4. The workspace and the bundle

- Time: 40 minutes
- Course objective: LO5
- The bundle is the set of files that carry the product: spec, UI mock note, technical note, test note, and a knowledge note.
- Learners sketch the folder map on paper, then create that map in the prepared workspace.
- They place a stub in each file so Module 5 fills files instead of inventing locations.
- The map is done when a partner can point to the file that answers "what must the slice do?" and "how will we know?"

## Module 5. One slice, end to end, with skills

- Time: 150 minutes
- Course objective: LO6
- The facilitator runs a timed path. Learners stay on the path. Each step has a file and a skill.

1. Spec: two acceptance checks in the spec file, written so a person can pass or fail them without asking the author.
2. ATDD: turn those checks into the test note before any UI work.
3. UI: a mock of the one screen, with the fields the checks name.
4. Technical design: a short note of the page, the data, and what is out of scope.
5. Test: run the checks and record pass or fail.
6. Implementation: use the Lite skill to build the page until both checks pass.

- The facilitator pauses after the spec and after the first failing test. Those two pauses are the teaching points.
- The module is complete when the slice runs and both checks pass, or when one check fails and the learner can point to the spec line it violates.

## Module 6. Knowledge, rules, and a workflow choice

- Time: 55 minutes
- Course objectives: LO7, LO8
- Learners add one knowledge note the product needs (a term, a policy limit, or a source of truth).
- They attach a rule: the agent uses that note and does not invent a second definition.
- They ask for the term in a fresh prompt and show that the answer comes from the note.
- They then compare two workflow patterns for "spec, then test, then build":
  - A fixed skill sequence a person starts one step at a time
  - An agent that may choose the next step inside the same rules
- Each learner writes a recommendation for their real team: the pattern, one constraint (review load, risk, or skill of the team), and the reason.
- Pairs read the recommendation aloud in one minute. The room does not need one shared answer.

## What the facilitator prepares

- Laptops with the Lite skills and rules installed
- The class-product starter workspace and the folder map
- The six sorting cards for Module 3
- A completed sample slice learners can open if they stall after the first failing test
- A clock for the two pauses in Module 5
